# Valgrind suppressions for AML's test suite.
#
# `make test-valgrind` treats "definitely lost" and "indirectly lost" as
# failures. GLib, GDK, Pango, Fontconfig, GTK and the graphics driver
# allocate one-time caches and per-thread state that outlive the process
# by design, and the test binaries exit without running the toolkit's
# teardown. Those reports say nothing about AML's own ownership, so they
# are listed here.
#
# Two rules keep this file from hiding real leaks:
#
#   1. Every entry is anchored on a leaf frame inside the toolkit, never on
#      an AML symbol. A leak in AML cannot match any of them.
#   2. AML's own leaks are never suppressed, so a real direct or indirect
#      leak still fails the run. See the note on `indirect` below.
#
# Why `indirect` is suppressed alongside `definite`: Valgrind works out
# what is indirectly lost relative to the blocks it is actually going to
# report. Suppress a root and everything hanging off it is re-parented
# into "indirectly lost", which then fails the run on its own. Leaving
# `indirect` out would defeat every entry here, and would say nothing new:
# if the only root of a group is one we have decided is the toolkit's,
# its children are the toolkit's too.
#
# The `...` lines matter: the toolkit ships stripped with inlined helpers,
# so the allocation stack always carries frames Valgrind prints as `???`.
# Suppression frames must otherwise be consecutive, and without the
# wildcard none of these would ever match.

# GLib allocates the per-thread default main context inside the worker
# thread the first time a GTask completes there, and never frees it.
{
   glib-worker-main-context
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_get_worker_context*
}

# The same context again, pushed explicitly: GLib's GDBus thread installs
# a thread-default main context when it starts and does not pop it before
# the thread ends, so the queue it holds is reported lost.
{
   glib-thread-default-main-context
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_main_context_push_thread_default*
}

# The dispatch loop caches the source currently being run.
{
   glib-main-context-current-source
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_main_current_source
}

# The same cache, reached through the dispatch loop rather than through
# the accessor: GLib builds the current-source record inside an inlined
# helper, so the dispatcher is the only named frame on the stack.
{
   glib-main-context-dispatch
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_main_dispatch*
}

# GTask completion on a thread-pool thread keeps the thread's main context
# alive for the rest of the process.
{
   glib-task-worker-context
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_task_get_type*
}

# GLib's thread pool creates threads whose TLS blocks are reported
# "possibly lost" once the thread has exited.
{
   glib-thread-pool-tls
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   ...
   fun:g_thread_pool_*
}

# GTK resolves its default style sheet once per process and caches the
# resolved colour values in it.
{
   gtk-css-style-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:gtk_css_*
}

# GObject property bindings installed by GSettings cache boxes that live
# as long as the type, which is past the end of the test process.
{
   gsettings-binding-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_binding_init
}

# The GDK file-transfer portal probe opens a GDBus connection during
# gtk_init(), whose threads and TLS blocks outlive the process.
{
   gdk-portal-dbus
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:file_transfer_portal_register*
}

# Pango caches the fontconfig patterns it matches, and Fontconfig caches
# the result of each match, for the life of the process. A test that puts
# real text on screen leaves megabytes of them behind. AML hands Pango its
# own strings and Pango copies what it needs, so none of AML's memory is
# in here.
{
   pango-fontconfig-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:pango_*
}

# Fontconfig's own pattern objects, reached from Pango's font map.
{
   fontconfig-pattern-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:Fc*
}

# GTK builds its Vulkan/OpenGL shader pipelines the first time anything is
# drawn. The graphics driver allocates them and nothing frees them before
# the process exits.
{
   gtk-renderer-pipeline-cache
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,indirect
   ...
   fun:gsk_*
}

# GIO unloads the dconf settings module after its worker has exited. The
# module's loader bookkeeping is then reported as a small definite leak;
# it is outside AML and only appears when the dconf module is available.
{
   gio-dconf-module-unload
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:g_io_extension_point_get_extensions
}

# The Arch Duktape build uses realloc(ptr, 0) while compiling its embedded
# scripts. Memcheck diagnoses that library-internal call even though the
# resulting allocation is handled by Duktape; do not hide calls from AML.
{
   duktape-realloc-zero
   Memcheck:Param
   realloc(size)
   fun:realloc
   ...
   obj:/usr/lib/libduktape.so.*
}
